! mcp_bootstrap_p186.strap -- MCP printed page 186: the UNIT NOT READY
! REJECT routine (K UNR) and the channel/unit-number-to-IQS converter
! (K CONV), with the two operator-message cards they fill in.
!
! docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF p.193.  The header was READ, not
! assumed, at 400 dpi:  "TIME CLOCK 00054041   PUNID 11D11MCP
! CARD ID LE000053   DIST. OF 3/01   3/01   PAGE NUMBER  186".
!
! >>> THE COLUMN-HEADING ROW'S OWN "LOCATION" FIELD READS 221306. <<<
! Six digits, printed after the word LOCATION at the right-hand end
! of the heading line, clear of the dashed rule.  That is LINE 1'S
! location word (docs/transcription_workflow.md step 1), and line 1
! here IS a located row -- the numbered case the rule predicts.
! Line 1's own LOCATION column reads 221306.40.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE PAGE IS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! FIFTY-ONE PRINTED LINES, serials LE000053..LE000097.  Nine of them
! are comment cards (10-14, 34-37) carrying a "-" in the NAME column
! and no location.  No SLC, no SYN, NO RESERVATION OF ANY KIND.
! The page is CONTIGUOUS from 221306.40 to 221350.40 -- thirty-three
! words and a half, no hole -- and then blank paper to the foot.
!
!   lines  1-9    the tail of the end-of-operation wait: K SEOUL
!                 loops on the control word's SEOP bit, gives up to
!                 K REJT after three EKJ's, and K CBJ R returns to
!                 K RETRY once the control word is cleared.
!   lines 10-14   the banner and the calling sequence for what
!                 follows.
!   lines 15-30   K UNR, the unit-not-ready arm: convert the channel
!                 and unit numbers (K CONV), set the commentator's
!                 busy/wait flags, call the commentator with the
!                 message K SERV M, then K K LOOP waits for the unit
!                 to come ready and K UNR R returns.
!   lines 31-33   the routine's data: the index word K K CCW and the
!                 two IQS message cards K SERV M and K NN.
!   lines 34-37   the second banner.
!   lines 38-51   K CONV: expand the channel number to 8-bit bytes,
!                 make it an IQS numeral, store it into the message,
!                 do the same for the unit number, and return
!                 through K CONV R.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE SPAN, ESTABLISHED FROM THE PAGE AND NOT FROM THE SURVEY
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! The brief no longer quotes survey endpoints, so this deck fixed
! its own.  Measured three ways that agree:
!
!   * the LINE column runs 1..51 with no gap, and the page's text
!     rows were located mechanically (ink-row detection over the
!     NAME+STATEMENT columns at 1200 dpi): fifty-one rows of body
!     text, the last at 221350.00, and nothing but dashed rule and
!     blank paper below it.
!   * the CARD-SERIAL column runs LE000053..LE000097 with the usual
!     one-card-two-lines repeats (53, 57, 65, 69, 70, 74 each print
!     twice) and NO gap.  Forty-five distinct serials for fifty-one
!     lines.
!   * the counter chain closes: 221306.40 + the printed widths =
!     221350.40, and the last located row prints 221350.00.
!
! strap/mcp_ocr.py reads only 15 of the 51 locations on this page,
! and the fifteen it reads all agree with the eye (its one wobble is
! "221314.46" for line 15's 221314.40).  That is the cross-check the
! workflow asks for, not a source.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE JOIN, AND WHY THIS DECK'S ORIGIN IS CHECKED AND NOT ASSERTED
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! The page prints NO SLC, so the SLC below is this deck's own.  But
! it is NOT an assertion: FIVE landed decks already SYN symbols that
! THIS page defines, and strap/mcp_image.merge compares them --
!
!     KCBJR   221313.40   pp.125, 126, 184   (line  9)
!     KKLOOP  221321.40   p.184              (line 24)
!     KUNRR   221325.40   p.184              (line 30)
!     KCONV   221335.00   pp.146, 179        (line 38)
!     KCONVR  221350.00   pp.146, 179        (line 51)
!
! -- and all five of those are read off SIC or branch half words on
! pages that had never seen this one.  Moving this deck's origin one
! half word and re-merging is refused (the injection was run; see
! the report).  So the origin is CHECKED.
!
! The join backwards is independent as well: printed p.185's LAST
! line is a full-word "BZB,SMULTI($10),KSEOUL" at 221305.40, which
! leaves the counter at 221306.40 -- this page's line 1.  That card
! also REFERENCES this page's line 3, and prints 221307.74 for it:
! 0o40 OR 0o34 = 0o74, so p.185 reads K SEOUL at 221307.40, which is
! the location line 3 prints.  Two pages, one symbol, same answer.
! The CARD SERIALS close the same join: p.185's last line is
! LE000052 and this page's line 1 is LE000053.
!
! FORWARD: line 51's "B,$" is a HALF WORD at 221350.00, so the
! counter leaves this page at 221350.40 and printed p.187 must begin
! there.  Stated as a prediction and then checked: printed p.187's
! deck already carries "SLC,(8)221350.40", and its CARD ID header
! reads LF000001 against this page's last serial LE000097 -- a new
! serial family at the page break, which is the usual pattern.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! GLYPH CALLS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! (1) LINE 3's NAME IS "K SEOUL", WITH A LETTER O.  The round glyph
!     is settled against the drum's own Q and D on the same page:
!     line 32's "(IQSX)" prints a Q with a plain tail and its "DD"
!     prints D with a straight left stem and squared corners, and
!     this glyph has neither.  That leaves O / C / 0, and the C is
!     excluded by the two known C's of line 3's OWN comment,
!     "-CHECK": their right-hand gap is wide and this glyph's is a
!     hairline break in an otherwise continuous bowl.  printed
!     p.185's reference to the same symbol prints it CLOSED, on a
!     row whose "($10)" prints a known 0 OPEN -- so the page that
!     references it and the page that defines it print the same
!     letter and neither prints a C.
!     NOT SETTLED BY SHAPE ALONE, and this is recorded as a call:
!     the workflow's p.184 measurement says width and openness do
!     not separate O/0/C.  What is offered here is the Q and D
!     EXCLUSION (which is a shape argument that measurement does not
!     cover) plus the C/O contrast against known C's in the same
!     row.  The volume OCR reads "KSECUL" all three times it sees
!     the name, which is the same ink read worse and is not a vote.
!     THE NAME OCCURS NOWHERE ELSE IN THE CORPUS -- three hits, all
!     on pp.185/186 -- so no manual and no other page can settle it.
!     If printed p.185's transcriber reads it differently, THAT is
!     the disagreement to resolve, and it is a real one: the merge
!     will leave the symbol unresolved rather than flag it.
!     >>> COMPARED, AND WE AGREE. <<<  printed p.185's deck (its
!     GLYPH CALL (1)) independently reads KSEOUL and SYNs it at
!     221307.40, and its argument is a different one from the one
!     above and worth more: the listing OCR renders the TYPESET-
!     confirmed sister symbol KSEOPT -- same K S E O prefix -- as
!     "K SECP T" ten times against two for "K SEOP", so the
!     reader's O->C substitution is MEASURED on a name whose
!     spelling a press settles; and the SEOP family is spelled with
!     an O on printed p.99's "S SEOP SYN(BU,1),0.46".  Two pages,
!     two independent arguments, one spelling.  It is still a call.
!
! (2) LINE 9's NAME IS "K CBJ R", WITH A C.  CONFIRMED, and not from
!     this page's ink: pp.125 and 126 both SYN KCBJR at 221313.40,
!     which is the location this page prints for it.  printed p.184
!     asked for this to be checked; it checks out.
!
! (3) "K UNR" (line 15, 221314.00) AND "K UNR R" (line 30,
!     221325.40) ARE TWO DIFFERENT SYMBOLS.  CONFIRMED off this
!     page's ink and arithmetic both: the two NAME fields are four
!     and six characters wide in the measured character grid, the
!     two locations are twelve half words apart, line 15's SIC/B
!     pair ENTERS the routine and line 30's "B,$" is where it
!     RETURNS to, and p.184 line 22's "SIC,K UNR R" prints
!     221325.40 -- line 30's location, not line 15's.
!
! (4) >>> THE CALLING-SEQUENCE SEPARATOR IS ONE CHARACTER, AND IT IS
!     A RECORD MARK, NOT "**".  printed p.184's deck reports the
!     line-13 comment as "SIC,K UNR R ** B,K UNR".
!     REFUTED BY MEASUREMENT FIRST: this page's character grid (a
!     least-squares fit of a 40.09 px cell at 400 dpi, every glyph
!     within 2.3 px) puts "R", the separator, "B", "," and "K" in
!     FIVE CONSECUTIVE CELLS.  The separator occupies exactly one
!     cell, so it cannot be "**" (two cells) and cannot be " ** "
!     (four).  Line 35 measures the same.
!     AND THE CHARACTER IS IDENTIFIED: at 1600 dpi it is a single
!     vertical stroke with TWO horizontal crossbars -- the IBM
!     record mark -- which looks nothing like the six-point stars
!     the real "**" prints earlier in the same comment.
!     This changes no symbol and no bit; it is recorded because a
!     later reader comparing the two decks would otherwise see a
!     contradiction.  Written as "#" in the listing block above.
!
! (5) THE COMMENT ON LINE 13 IS LINE 13, NOT LINE 12.  printed
!     p.184's deck cites "p.186 line 12's comment".  Line 12 is a
!     blank comment card (serial LE000062); the calling sequence is
!     line 13 (LE000063).  An off-by-one in a citation, nothing more.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE TWO MESSAGE CARDS, AND WHAT SETTLES THEIR CHARACTER COUNTS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Lines 32 and 33 print NOTHING in the BINARY OUTPUT column, so no
! !CHECK can test them directly.  Their character counts are settled
! by ARITHMETIC, and the arithmetic is sharp:
!
!   K SERV M  221327.00 -> K NN 221333.00   = 4 words = 256 bits
!   K NN      221333.00 -> line 38 221335.00 = 2 words = 128 bits
!
! At eight bits a character that is 32 and 16 characters exactly.
! The character positions were measured, not eyeballed: the print is
! a 10-per-inch monospace grid, and a least-squares fit of cell
! centres over each row lands every glyph within 2.3 px of a 40.09
! px cell at 400 dpi.  Counting cells from the comma:
!
!   line 32   cells 23..54 are data, cell 55 is "X"   -> 32 chars
!   line 33   cells 23..38 are data, cell 39 is "Y"   -> 16 chars
!
! >>> THE TRAILING X AND Y ARE THE ENTRY MODE'S OWN TERMINATOR, NOT
! >>> DATA.  "(IQSX)" is mode IQS terminated by X and "(IQSY)" is
! mode IQS terminated by Y, exactly as p.184's four "(A*)" cards are
! mode A terminated by "*".  Counting the X as data gives 33
! characters = 264 bits, which would put K NN at 221334.00; the page
! prints 221333.00.  So the arithmetic decides it, and it decides it
! the same way on both cards.
!
! >>> AND THE PAGE STATES THE ANSWER A SECOND TIME, IN THE CODE. <<<
! Line 23 is the second parameter of the commentator call, ",6.",
! and its comment reads "-LOCATION + LENGTH OF MESSAGE".  Line 22
! passes the LOCATION (K SERV M, 221327.00) and line 23 passes the
! LENGTH: SIX WORDS.  K SERV M is four words (0o327->0o333) and K NN
! is two (0o333->0o335), and four plus two is the six the card
! carries.  So the two message cards together are six words = 384
! bits = 48 characters at eight bits, which is 32 + 16 -- the counts
! the terminator reading gives.  Count the X and the Y as data and
! the message is 50 characters and the card should say 7.  THIS IS
! INDEPENDENT OF THE LOCATION COUNTER: it is what the program says
! about its own data, not what the assembler did with it.
!
! The trailing blanks ARE data -- line 32's datum ends in a blank at
! cell 54 and line 33's in six blanks at cells 33-38 -- and they are
! the positions the converter writes the IQS numerals into.  That is
! the workflow's "in entry mode the space after the comma is DATA"
! rule working at the END of the datum.
!
! WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: the CHARACTER COUNTS are checked three ways
! (the counter, the grid, and line 23's "6."), but the LETTERS are
! read off ink alone and nothing in docs/ocr/ prints this message.
! The nearest typeset witness is the JUNE 1964 Reference Manual
! C22-6678-1 Appendix D, "$OPTR READY CHXX UNY." -- a LATER
! revision of the same message, which corroborates the sense and
! the X/Y channel-and-unit placeholders and settles no character.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE LOCATION-COLUMN ASTERISK: LINES 3, 26 AND 40
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Three rows carry it, measured (a 27 px glyph 53 px to the right of
! the last location digit, on a page whose character pitch is 40):
!
!   line  3   221307.40 *   K SEOUL  LCI,1,K REP
!   line 26   221323.00 *            BZEKJZ,$+1.32
!   line 40   221336.40 *            LVE,1,K CONV R
!
! RECORDED, NOT EXPLAINED.  This page kills at least one hypothesis
! on its own: lines 5 and 26 are the SAME STATEMENT, "BZEKJZ,$+1.32",
! and only line 26 carries it, so it is not a property of the
! operation.  It is not "the row is a label" either (line 26 and
! line 40 are unnamed, and the page's seven other named rows do not
! carry it).  Per the brief, no rule is proposed from one page.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> ONE ROW FAILS, ON PURPOSE: LINE 47. <<<
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> RESOLVED 2026-11-02 -- strap_asm WAS WRONG AND IS FIXED. <<<
! This block is kept as the record of what the failing row bought.
! The gate is the operand's SPELLING: an ADDRESS is now deposited
! whole into the 24-bit field and the HARDWARE takes the leftmost
! L bits, exactly as Reference Manual printed p.56 says -- "the
! effective address itself is used as the operand.  The field
! length specifies the number of bits in the field starting with
! the LEFTMOST bit of the effective address."  A plain integer
! still packs left-justified by length (127 -> 127<<17), so no
! landed deck moved.  Both rows below now assemble as printed.
!
! THE PAGE AND strap_asm DISAGREED ABOUT A VFL-IMMEDIATE'S A24 FIELD
! WHEN THE FIELD LENGTH IS SHORTER THAN THE OPERAND, and this page is
! the discriminating case because it writes THE SAME IMMEDIATE TWICE
! AT TWO LENGTHS:
!
!   line 43  CI0111(BU,16),(8)300600.   prints  300600.00 80 420000.16 70
!   line 47  CI0111(BU,8), (8)300600.   prints  300600.00 80 410000.16 70
!
! The two second half words differ by exactly 0o10000, which is the
! LENGTH field: 0o020000 is 16 and 0o010000 is 8, the same scale
! lines 45 and 48 print for CM0101 at those two lengths.  So the
! lengths are not in doubt.  THE FIRST HALF WORD IS IDENTICAL on the
! two rows -- rendered at 1200 dpi both read 300600.00, digit for
! digit, and the "6" is unmistakable against the 0's beside it.
!
! strap_asm assembles line 47 as 300000.00 80.  It converts the
! bit-address term to OPERAND UNITS (2**(24-length), so 2**16 here),
! truncating, and repacks: 0o30060000 >> 16 = 0o140 (= 0x60), back
! <<16 = 0o30000000.  Measured over lengths, everything else fixed:
!
!     L= 4  300000.00 80       L=16  300600.00 80
!     L= 8  300000.00 80       L=18  300600.00 80
!     L=12  300600.00 80       L=24  300600.00 80
!
! -- the quantisation only shows once the unit passes the operand's
! lowest set bit, which is why line 43 passes and line 47 does not,
! and why no landed page has caught it: it needs one immediate used
! at two lengths on one page.  IBM's assembler evidently deposited
! the whole 24-bit effective address and left the hardware to take
! the leftmost L bits (which is what the code wants: 0o300600. is
! the IQS zone pair 0x6060, and its top 8 bits are the single zone
! byte 0x60 that line 47 needs).
!
! PER docs/transcription_workflow.md 3a2 AND THE BRIEF, THE PRINTED
! STATEMENT AND THE PRINTED CHECK BOTH STAY AND THE ROW FAILS.
! Writing a statement that reproduces 300000.00 would verify this
! deck against itself and hide the defect.  The other thirty-nine
! !CHECKs pass and all ten symbols land on their printed locations,
! so nothing else on the page rests on this.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! ASSEMBLER: ONE REFUSAL, TWICE.  Every one of the forty-two
! statements was handed to strap_asm IN ITS PRINTED FORM first.
! Lines 8 and 29 were REFUSED -- "undefined symbol 'K'" -- because
! they put a name CONTAINING A BLANK inside an EXPRESSION
! ("K K CCW+.24", "K K CCW.18"), the gap the brief names.  The
! refusal is an AssemblerError and aborts the WHOLE assembly, so
! unlike a wrong word it cannot be left to fail: the blanks are
! closed up to "KKCCW" at both occurrences and flagged there.  It is
! the same symbol the deck's own label defines, so no reading
! changed.  The printed forms are in the listing block above.
! Everything else was accepted as printed, including the two
! blank-operation cards, the "(IQSX)"/"(IQSY)" entry modes, the
! bare-decimal bit offsets (".24", ".18", ".56", ".63", "1.32"),
! the index-named operands ($1, $12, $14) and the VF.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------

KSTCS   SYN, (8)216272.40            ! line  1 SIC -- BIT-EXACT; landed p.122
KCSIN   SYN, (8)216267.0             ! line  2 B; bit offset unobservable; printed p.128 line 36 is the defining card at 216267.00
KREP    SYN(BU,18), 30               ! lines 3, 24 -- A COUNT, not an address.
!                                      dds carried from the DEFINING card,
!                                      printed p.185 line 27 "K REP SYN(BU,18),30";
!                                      the merge refuses without it
KREJT   SYN, (8)221300.0             ! lines 7, 28 B; landed pp.125, 157
KRETRY  SYN, (8)221263.0             ! line  9 B; bit offset unobservable.  printed p.185 line 13 is the defining card, "K RETRY LX,0,SIOINS($12)" at 221263.00
JIOBSY  SYN, (8)233666.04            ! line 18 VFL address -- BIT-EXACT; landed p.184, no dds
JWAIT   SYN, (8)233666.00            ! line 19 VFL address -- BIT-EXACT; landed p.184, no dds
SCOMIC  SYN, (8)215470.40            ! line 20 SIC -- BIT-EXACT; landed pp.116, 179
SCOMM   SYN, (8)215470.0             ! line 21 B; landed pp.116, 179
SCHANN  SYN(BU,7), 0.12              ! line 39 -- landed by four decks, which cite printed p.101 line 5 as the defining card; the dds is carried, not invented
SUNITN  SYN(BU,3), 0.47              ! line 46 -- landed by four decks, which cite printed p.101 line 6 as the defining card; the dds is carried, not invented

        SLC, (8)221306.40            ! NOT PRINTED.  CHECKED by the merge; see THE JOIN
        SIC,K ST CS                  ! line  1
        B,K CS IN                    ! line  2   -STACK CHANNEL SIGNAL
KSEOUL  LCI,1,K REP                  ! line  3 * -CHECK FOR END OP OF REL
        CCW,0.0($14),K K CCW         ! line  4
        BZEKJZ,$+1.32                ! line  5
        CB,1,$-1.32                  ! line  6
        B,K REJT                     ! line  7   -IF 3 EKJ, GIVE UP
        BB,KKCCW+.24,K SEOUL         ! line  8   -IF SEOP BIT ON, LOOP
!   >>> PRINTED "BB,K K CCW+.24,K SEOUL".  strap_asm REFUSES a blank
!   inside a name INSIDE AN EXPRESSION ("undefined symbol K"), and
!   the refusal aborts the WHOLE assembly, so the blanks are closed
!   up here.  Same symbol, same bits; see ASSEMBLER in the report.
KCBJR   B,K RETRY                    ! line  9   -CTL WORD CLEARED FOR RETRY
KUNR    SIC,K CONV R                 ! line 15
        B,K CONV                     ! line 16   -CONVERT CHAN + UNIT NO TO IQS
        VF,K NN                      ! line 17
        LF(BU,1),JIOBSY              ! line 18
        SF(BU,1),JWAIT               ! line 19
        SIC,S COMIC                  ! line 20
        B,S COMM                     ! line 21   -CALL COMMENTATOR
        ,K SERV M                    ! line 22   blank operation
        ,6.                          ! line 23   -LOCATION + LENGTH OF MESSAGE
KKLOOP  LCI,1,K REP                  ! line 24   -WAIT FOR UNIT TO BECOME READY
        CCW,0.0($14),K K CCW         ! line 25
        BZEKJZ,$+1.32                ! line 26 *
        CB,1,$-1.32                  ! line 27
        B,K REJT                     ! line 28   -IF REPEATED EKJ, GIVE UP
        BZB,KKCCW.18,K K LOOP        ! line 29   -CHECK UNR STATUS BIT
!   >>> PRINTED "BZB,K K CCW.18,K K LOOP".  Same refusal, same
!   closing-up, same bits.
KUNRR   B,$                          ! line 30   -IF UNR ON, ALLS WELL
KKCCW   XW,0                         ! line 31
! lines 32 and 33: the terminator is the entry mode's own X and Y.
! The blanks before the terminator are DATA.  No trailing comment.
KSERVM  (IQSX)DD(BU,64),*** OPERATOR ***  READY CHANNEL X
KNN     (IQSY)DD(BU,64),   UNIT  .      Y
KCONV   SX,1,K K CCW                 ! line 38   -SAVE XR1
        LCV(BU,7),S CHAN N($12)      ! line 39   -IF NOT,SEND MESSAGE
        LVE,1,K CONV R               ! line 40 *
        LF(BU,8,4),$R.56,1           ! line 41   -EXPAND IT TO 8 BIT BYTES
        CM0000(BU,1),$R.63           ! line 42
        CI0111(BU,16),(8)300600.     ! line 43   -MAKE IT IQS NUMERAL
        LV,1,0.0($1)                 ! line 44
        CM0101(BU,16),0.0($1)        ! line 45
        L(BU,3),S UNIT N($12),1      ! line 46   -PICK UP UNIT NO
        CI0111(BU,8),(8)300600.      ! line 47
        CM0101(BU,8),1.0($1)         ! line 48
        LX,1,K K CCW                 ! line 49   -RESTORE XR1
        M+1(BU,19),K CONV R          ! line 50   -SET RETURN ADDRESS
KCONVR  B,$                          ! line 51

